{
  "case_id": "2008-08-108",
  "document_hash": "637153a33f31fbd7c0801618d3bd4a87fb7e26dd004bfdf194951574d0ffd4e0",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2008-08-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "637153a33f31fbd7c0801618d3bd4a87fb7e26dd004bfdf194951574d0ffd4e0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-637153a33f31-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. RUSSELL: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court:\nIn common usage to say that somebody knowingly transfers, possesses or uses something is to say that that person knows what it is that he is transferring, possessing or using. If I say that John knowingly used a pair of scissors of his mother, I am saying not simply that John knew he was using something that turned out to be his mother's scissors or even that John knew he was using scissors which turned out to be his mother's, I am saying that John knew that the scissors he was using belonged out to be his mother."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2008-08-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "637153a33f31fbd7c0801618d3bd4a87fb7e26dd004bfdf194951574d0ffd4e0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-637153a33f31-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Doesn't that depend on the"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2008-08-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "637153a33f31fbd7c0801618d3bd4a87fb7e26dd004bfdf194951574d0ffd4e0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-637153a33f31-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Would it be enough to go to the jury on the hypothetical Justice Alito gives you?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2008-08-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "637153a33f31fbd7c0801618d3bd4a87fb7e26dd004bfdf194951574d0ffd4e0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-637153a33f31-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: What about the\ngovernment's argument in this case that Congress was\nreally going after people who have false identification\nbecause of its concern to protect the victim, that is\nthe person whose number is misused? So the government\nis urging that we take a victim-centered approach to the\nstatute."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2008-08-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "637153a33f31fbd7c0801618d3bd4a87fb7e26dd004bfdf194951574d0ffd4e0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-637153a33f31-0001-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: If I could just interrupt\nyou, why does \"of another individual\" -- why can't that\nbe read to mean \"of a person other than the person who\nis using the identification,\" whether this other person\nis real or not?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Heytens",
      "case_id": "2008-08-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "637153a33f31fbd7c0801618d3bd4a87fb7e26dd004bfdf194951574d0ffd4e0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-637153a33f31-0002-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "we do not contend that this statutory text standing along ambiguously supports our position enough to terminate the inquiry."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2008-08-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "637153a33f31fbd7c0801618d3bd4a87fb7e26dd004bfdf194951574d0ffd4e0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-637153a33f31-0002-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: Shouldn't it -- shouldn't it -- is it time to revisit the Court's decision in Hayes?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Heytens",
      "case_id": "2008-08-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "637153a33f31fbd7c0801618d3bd4a87fb7e26dd004bfdf194951574d0ffd4e0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-637153a33f31-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And where the argument lies is between did Congress do this by punishing people only who intend to engage in identity theft or people who, while not intending to do so, have that effect."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Russell",
      "case_id": "2008-08-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "637153a33f31fbd7c0801618d3bd4a87fb7e26dd004bfdf194951574d0ffd4e0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-637153a33f31-0002-0006",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 53
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Justice Breyer, you asked if there were examples of other statutes in which knowledge requirements didn't extend to all the elements, but the government gave two examples. The first, Morissette, is clearly an example with a jurisdictional element. All of the circuit courts that say that the knowledge requirement doesn't extend to, of the United States, do so on the grounds that because there is a jurisdictional elements and jurisdictional elements don't extend -- don't require mens rea."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Government",
      "case_id": "2008-08-108",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "637153a33f31fbd7c0801618d3bd4a87fb7e26dd004bfdf194951574d0ffd4e0",
      "evidence_id": "ev-637153a33f31-0003-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Finally, with respect to the rule of lenity, the government I think has acknowledged that the statutory text is at least ambiguous with respect to whether or not it compels their conclusion. They acknowledge that you can make policy arguments both ways about what would be a good idea about how to treat this kind of conduct, and I think regardless of your view of what the trigger of the rule of lenity is, this is a classic case for it."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Source extraction",
      "case_id": "2008-08-108",
